# Defanged reconstruction of a real malicious extension, for scanner tests.
# Never install or run. See internal/e2e/livescan/corpus.go.
id: glassworm-codejoy-1.8.3
title: "codejoy.codejoy-vscode-extension 1.8.3 (Open VSX): GlassWorm loader hidden in invisible Unicode characters"
campaign: glassworm-2025
date: "2025-10-17"
kind: vsix
ecosystem: openvsx
name: codejoy.codejoy-vscode-extension
version: "1.8.3"
benignVersion: "1.8.2"
summary: >-
  GlassWorm, named by Koi Security, was first seen on Open VSX on 2025-10-17:
  new versions of a handful of extensions were published from their real
  publishers' accounts (Eclipse says through publish tokens leaked in public
  repositories, which Wiz had reported; Koi says compromised developer
  accounts). Koi's risk engine flagged codejoy.codejoy-vscode-extension first:
  1.8.3 and 1.8.4 were the malicious CodeJoy releases, between the clean 1.8.2
  (2025-10-16) and 1.8.5 (2025-10-20). The JavaScript carried its loader as a
  run of thousands of invisible characters inside what looks like an empty
  template literal: Unicode variation selectors (U+FE00-FE0F and U+E0100-E01EF,
  256 values, one per byte; Endor Labs counted 6,492 in the sample it decoded)
  that a short function mapped back to bytes and passed to eval. The decoded
  stage polled the Solana blockchain for the newest transaction memo of a
  hard-coded wallet; the memo's JSON held a base64 link to the next stage on a
  plain IP address (a Google Calendar event title was a backup), which came back
  AES-encrypted with its key and IV in the HTTP response headers. That stage
  stole npm, GitHub, Open VSX and Git credentials, went for cryptocurrency
  wallet extensions (49 per Koi, more than 70 per Endor) and, Koi says, used the
  stolen tokens to publish further extensions; a last module added a SOCKS
  proxy, hidden VNC and peer-to-peer control channels. Koi's analysis began on
  2025-10-18; Open VSX removed the extensions, revoked or rotated the tokens and
  called the incident contained on 2025-10-21. Reports differ: Koi counted about
  35,800 downloads and called GlassWorm a self-propagating worm, while Eclipse
  says bots inflated the downloads and that nothing spread by itself beyond the
  stolen credentials.
techniques: [publisher-compromise, invisible-unicode, eval-loader, blockchain-c2, credential-theft, remote-access]
reconstruction: >-
  extension.js carries the loader as Aikido and OpenSourceMalware quote it for
  the later npm, GitHub and Open VSX waves, with the mapping Koi and Endor
  describe for the first one: a short function turns each variation selector
  back into a byte (U+FE00-FE0F to 0-15, U+E0100-E01EF to 16-255, anything else
  dropped) and the bytes, read as UTF-8, go to eval, from a template literal
  that shows as empty. The run is 1,163 characters, not the 6,492 Endor counted,
  because it decodes to a short stage that can do nothing: it keeps the call
  shape Koi shows (getSignaturesForAddress(WALLET, { limit: 1000 }), the memo
  filter and regular expression, atob on the memo's base64 link) but answers
  from a table inside the script, with a wallet string that is not a valid
  address, so the only address it builds is under next.invalid, which fetch can
  never reach. Dropped: the RPC lookup, the AES step with key and IV from the
  response headers, the calendar fallback, the stealer and the remote-access
  module. Endor reports that the first-wave run decodes to Base64 text that is
  decoded again before eval; here it decodes straight to the script. The
  activation cooldown and the OS-specific native decoders Endor describes are
  not reproduced. The rest of the extension (a status bar item and two commands)
  is a small stand-in; its manifest fields (main, activationEvents,
  engines.vscode, the two commands) follow the real clean 1.8.2. The generic
  corpus checks cannot see inside the run, so
  internal/e2e/livescan/corpus_glassworm_test.go decodes it and holds it to the
  same rules.
references:
  - https://www.paloaltonetworks.com/blog/security-operations/glassworm-first-self-propagating-worm-using-invisible-code-hits-openvsx-marketplace/
  - https://www.endorlabs.com/learn/invisible-threats-glassworm-unicode-vscode
  - https://blogs.eclipse.org/post/mika%C3%ABl-barbero/open-vsx-security-update-october-2025
  - https://www.truesec.com/hub/blog/glassworm-self-propagating-vscode-extension
  - https://thehackernews.com/2025/10/self-spreading-glassworm-infects-vs.html
  - https://www.aikido.dev/blog/glassworm-returns-unicode-attack-github-npm-vscode
  - https://opensourcemalware.com/blog/glassworm-invades-github-npm-open-vsx-and-vs-code
  - https://fluidattacks.com/blog/glassworm-vs-code-extensions-supply-chain-attack
legitimateHosts:
  - github.com
indicators:
  - "w >= 0xFE00 && w <= 0xFE0F"
  - "w >= 0xE0100 && w <= 0xE01EF"
  - "eval(Buffer.from(s("
extension:
  publisher: codejoy
  name: codejoy-vscode-extension
-- shared/readme.md --
# CodeJoy

Project management, deployment and workflow tools for VS Code.

## Features

- **Open Project Workflow** shows the steps of the workflow defined in
  `.codejoy/workflow.json`; without that file the steps are build, package and
  deploy.
- A status bar item shows the current workflow. Click it to open the workflow.
-- shared/workflow.js --
'use strict';

const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');

const DEFAULT_STEPS = ['build', 'package', 'deploy'];

// A project's workflow is the list of steps in .codejoy/workflow.json; a folder
// without one (or with one that is not valid) gets the default steps.
function loadWorkflow(folder) {
  const file = path.join(folder, '.codejoy', 'workflow.json');
  try {
    const data = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(file, 'utf8'));
    if (Array.isArray(data.steps) && data.steps.every((step) => typeof step === 'string')) {
      return { name: data.name || path.basename(folder), steps: data.steps };
    }
  } catch (err) {
    // fall through to the default
  }
  return { name: path.basename(folder), steps: DEFAULT_STEPS };
}

function describeWorkflow(workflow) {
  return workflow.name + ': ' + workflow.steps.join(' > ');
}

module.exports = { loadWorkflow, describeWorkflow };
